Ported parts. Each file names its source in its header.

- crates/quota-station-core/src/codex/config.rs
  from src-tauri/src/live/project/codex.rs: the reserved provider ids
  (openai, ollama, lmstudio) and the built-in ones; how a provider table's
  own credentials are told apart (env_key, experimental_bearer_token, and
  the header styles: auth, aws, headers, query_params); the
  requires_openai_auth rule (on for an environment or bearer key when a
  ChatGPT login exists, off otherwise); the refusal of a table with no key of
  its own; the placeholder CC Switch leaves in a dormant table; and the check
  that the active profile does not choose another provider.
- crates/quota-station-core/src/codex/login.rs
  from src-tauri/src/codex_config.rs: which auth.json fields count as a
  login, and the credential store modes (file, keyring, auto, ephemeral).
- crates/quota-station-core/src/codex/procs.rs
  from src-tauri/src/services/provider/codex_client_catalog.rs: telling
  Codex's shared background process (app-server --managed-daemon) from the
  other app-servers by its command line, and restarting it with
  codex app-server daemon restart under CODEX_HOME.

What was changed:

- Only the provider choice is switched. The app keeps no provider list or
  database of its own: the custom provider's id, address and key come from
  the existing config.toml, and no host, address or key is built in.
- The edit is made on the file's text with toml_edit, and only
  model_provider and the custom table's requires_openai_auth change; a check
  refuses any other change. auth.json and [mcp_servers] are never written,
  and the MCP list is never rebuilt.
- Each write checks a SHA-256 of the file the owner saw, keeps a backup
  (mode 600, the newest ten), re-reads before the rename and verifies after;
  a symlinked config.toml stays a symlink.
- The background process is restarted only after the owner ticks the box
  or confirms its own sheet; nothing is killed.

